KEY SYNTAXES — LEETCODE 895 FREQ STACK

1. HashMap basic operations

HashMap<Integer,Integer> m1 = new HashMap<>();

m1.containsKey(val)
→ checks if val exists

m1.put(val, 1)
→ adds/updates val

m1.get(val)
→ gets the value associated with val


2. HashMap with Stack as the value

HashMap<Integer,Stack<Integer>> m2 = new HashMap<>();

m2.put(f, new Stack<>())
→ creates a new Stack for frequency f

m2.get(f)
→ gets the Stack associated with frequency f

m2.get(f).push(val)
→ pushes val into that Stack

m2.get(f).pop()
→ removes and returns the top element


3. Stack operations

Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();

stack.push(val)
→ add to top

stack.pop()
→ remove and return top

stack.peek()
→ view top without removing

stack.isEmpty()
→ check whether empty


4. Updating a HashMap value

m1.put(val, m1.get(val) + 1)
→ increase frequency by 1

m1.put(val, m1.get(val) - 1)
→ decrease frequency by 1


5. Checking if a Stack inside a HashMap is empty

if(m2.get(maxf).isEmpty()){
    maxf--;
}

→ If the stack corresponding to maxf is empty,
  there are no more elements at that frequency.


6. Nested access

m2.get(maxf).pop()

Meaning:

m2
 ↓
get(maxf)
 ↓
returns Stack<Integer>
 ↓
pop()
 ↓
returns the top Integer


7. Generic HashMap syntax

HashMap<KeyType,ValueType>

Example:

HashMap<Integer,Integer>
→ Integer key → Integer value

HashMap<Integer,Stack<Integer>>
→ Integer key → Stack<Integer> value


8. Updating maximum

if(f > maxf){
    maxf = f;
}

→ Keep track of the highest frequency currently reached.
